# Supply chain at release time

### What can change under a release without a commit

Tutors Ladder, rung 7 (Shipped), talk 3

---

## Three things you did not write

A release runs code that is not in your repository:

| What | Named by | Can move without a commit? |
| --- | --- | --- |
| a GitHub Action | `uses: actions/checkout@v7` | yes: a tag is a pointer its owner can move |
| a base image | `FROM node:22-alpine` | yes: the registry moves the tag on every patch |
| a dependency | `"yaml": "^2.9.1"` | yes, until the lockfile pins it |

Each one runs with your release's privileges. The fix for each is the same idea: **name the bytes, not a
label**, and change the name only in a reviewed pull request.

---

## Actions, pinned by SHA

Every `uses:` in upstream's `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:

```yaml
- name: Checkout
  uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7
  with:
    persist-credentials: false

- name: Setup pnpm
  uses: pnpm/action-setup@ea17c68df8912ef543352723c149a84f56e3d413 # v6.1.0
```

A 40-character commit SHA cannot be moved. The comment keeps it readable. Upstream's
`.github/dependabot.yml` has a `github-actions` ecosystem, so updates arrive as pull requests that change
both.

Rung 7's template uses the same three SHAs, copied from upstream's `ci.yml`.

---

## Least privilege

```yaml
permissions:
  contents: read            # the whole of ci.yml's grant
```

- `persist-credentials: false`: the token is not left in `.git/config` for later steps
- a job that writes (an image push, a pin pull request) asks for exactly that scope, in its own workflow:
  `image-build.yml` grants `id-token: write` with the comment "keyless cosign signing"
- `deploy-pin-pr.yml` is *called*, not triggered by `workflow_run`, because "a workflow_run job runs with the
  base repository's write token whatever started the run (zizmor: dangerous-triggers)"

`zizmor.yml` runs **zizmor** over every workflow on every pull request.

---

## Rung 7 holds its workflow to the same rules

`tests/release/workflow.test.ts` reads `.github/workflows/ladder-ci.yml`. Pin one action by tag:

```
Pinning rule (zizmor: unpinned-uses): a tag such as @v4 can be moved to other code by whoever
controls the action, and your next run executes it with your token. Pin the full 40-character
commit SHA and keep the version in a comment for people (uses: actions/checkout@<sha> # v7),
as upstream's workflows do.
```

Its other rules: permissions declared and read-only; `persist-credentials: false`; no
`pull_request_target` or `workflow_run`; no `${{ github.event... }}` inside `run:`; a timeout on every job.

It reads text. It is not zizmor, and a real repository should run zizmor too.

---

## One required check

```yaml
# upstream .github/workflows/ci.yml (abridged)
ci-success:
  name: CI success
  if: always()
  needs: [build-and-test, platform-conformance, container-smoke, ...]   # sixteen jobs
  steps:
    - name: Every job succeeded
      env:
        RESULTS: ${{ join(needs.*.result, ' ') }}
      run: |
        for result in $RESULTS; do
          if [ "$result" != "success" ]; then exit 1; fi
        done
```

"The one check to mark as required in branch protection. A skipped or cancelled job counts as a failure."
The **Single Gate**, for CI. Rung 7's test fails if a job is missing from the gate's `needs`.

---

## Base images, pinned by digest

```dockerfile
# upstream Dockerfile
# node-pinned: the Node base image, pinned by digest (Rule 0667). A floating tag
# changes Node and Debian under the same commit whenever Docker Hub moves it:
# on 2026-10-22 node 22.23.3 -> 22.23.4 reached all four images with no PR.
FROM docker.io/library/node:26.13.1-bookworm-slim@sha256:0042e17d… AS node-pinned
```

Dependabot's `docker` ecosystem bumps the digest in its own pull request. `ARG NODE_IMAGE` lets a build use
a mirror; the digest still decides what is pulled.

---

## Rung 7's image

```dockerfile
FROM ${REGISTRY}/node:22.23.5-alpine@sha256:1314fb30… AS build
...
RUN node apps/cli/src/main.ts /ladder/fixtures/ladder-sample-course /site

FROM ${REGISTRY}/busybox:1.37.0@sha256:a7dbc319… AS runtime
LABEL org.opencontainers.image.version="${VERSION}" org.opencontainers.image.revision="${GIT_SHA}"
COPY --from=build /site /site
USER 10001:10001
CMD ["httpd", "-f", "-p", "8080", "-h", "/site"]
```

About 7 MB: no Node, no package manager at run time. `pnpm container:smoke` runs it `--read-only
--cap-drop ALL`, as upstream's `scripts/checks/container-smoke.ts` runs its images, and asks
`/version.json` which commit it is.

---

## Signed, by digest, before any tag

From the header of upstream's `image-build.yml`:

> The image is pushed by digest, with no tag; that digest is signed with cosign (keyless, GitHub OIDC)
> and carries an SPDX SBOM attestation and a SLSA v1 build provenance attestation, and only then are the
> tags above pointed at it, so no tag is ever visible unsigned.

- **Keyless**: no private key to steal. The certificate names the workflow, `image-build.yml@<ref>`.
- Anyone can check it: `cosign verify --certificate-identity-regexp ... --certificate-oidc-issuer
  https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com`.

---

## SBOM and provenance

| Attestation | Made by | Says |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SPDX SBOM | `anchore/sbom-action`, then `cosign attest --type spdxjson` | every package in the image |
| SLSA v1 provenance | a predicate written with `jq` from the run's own context, then `cosign attest --type slsaprovenance1` | which workflow built it, from which commit, in which run |

The provenance is written in-house "so no third-party action sees the registry credentials", and verified
with `cosign verify-attestation` before any tag points at the digest. The release harness checks all three
(signature, SBOM, provenance) on every image it pulls.

---

## Scanning, and saying "not affected"

- **Trivy** scans the amd64 image before anything is pushed: CRITICAL and HIGH findings with a fix stop the
  workflow. A promoted candidate is scanned **again**: a fix published since the rc must not be skipped.
- **OpenVEX**: `release/openvex.json` states advisories that cannot affect this release. The harness passes
  it to its scanner with `--vex`. A `not_affected` statement needs a justification such as
  `vulnerable_code_not_in_execute_path`; an impact statement alone is not enough.

At the pinned commit the file has **no statements**: nothing has been waived.

Also on every PR: CodeQL (`codeql.yml`) and OpenSSF Scorecard (`scorecard.yml`).

---

## What rung 7 keeps, and what it leaves

| Kept, and checked | Left for the Summit |
| --- | --- |
| actions pinned by SHA, with a test | running zizmor itself |
| read-only permissions, one gate | CodeQL, Scorecard |
| base images pinned by digest, with a test | Dependabot updating the pins |
| non-root user, read-only run, revision label | cosign signing (needs a registry and OIDC) |
| `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `--frozen-lockfile` in CI | SBOM and SLSA provenance attestations |
| no runtime dependency at all (the fence) | Trivy, OpenVEX |

The smallest supply chain is the one you do not have: rung 7's image runs no third-party package.

---

## Takeaways

- Pin **actions by SHA** and **images by digest**. A tag is a label someone else can move.
- Grant **read** by default; a write grant lives in its own workflow, with a reason.
- Upstream signs **by digest, before tagging**, and attaches an **SBOM** and **SLSA provenance** to it.
- **Trivy** stops known, fixable vulnerabilities; **OpenVEX** records, with evidence, the ones that cannot
  reach you.
- Rung 7 checks the first two in its gate. The rest needs a registry and an identity: that is the Summit.
